---
import RouteDemo from "../../components/town/RouteDemo";
import baseline from "../../../reports/portfolio-baseline.json";
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import CaseFlow from "../../components/CaseFlow.astro";
import { winterTownCaseStudy as study } from "../../data/winterTownCaseStudy";
import { profile } from "../../components/town/profile";
import "../../styles/case-study.css";
import "../../styles/content-layout.css";
import "../../components/town/project-demo.css";
const source = "https://github.com/j4ckwinter/winterbot/blob/486dfa1";
const homepageRuns = baseline.results.filter((run) => run.path === "/");
const range = (field: "fcpMs" | "observedLcpMs") =>
  `${Math.min(...homepageRuns.map((run) => run[field]))}–${Math.max(...homepageRuns.map((run) => run[field]))} ms`;
---

<Layout
  title="Building Winter Town — Jack Winter"
  description={study.summary}
  canonicalPath={study.href}
>
  <div class="case-study content-page">
    <nav class="case-nav content-page-nav" aria-label="Case study navigation">
      <a href="/portfolio#projects">← Projects</a>
      <a href="/">Explore the town ↗</a>
    </nav>
    <main id="main">
      <header class="case-intro content-page-intro">
        <p class="case-eyebrow">Winter Town · Personal project</p>
        <h1>{study.title}</h1>
        <p class="case-lead">{study.summary}</p>
        <dl class="case-overview" aria-label="Case study overview">
          <div>
            <dt>Problem</dt>
            <dd>
              Make exploration enjoyable without making visitors play a game to
              find my work.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>My role</dt>
            <dd>
              Visual design, frontend architecture, movement, interactions and
              browser testing.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Result</dt>
            <dd>
              A walkable town and a plain portfolio, sharing content and linking
              directly to individual projects.
            </dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </header>
      <figure class="town-study-preview">
        <img
          src="/previews/winter-town.png"
          width="960"
          height="640"
          alt="Winter Town: paths connect the workshop, house, station and post office."
        />
        <figcaption>
          The buildings give the portfolio a setting; direct links keep the
          information accessible.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <section class="case-prose" aria-labelledby="access-title">
        <h2 id="access-title">Two ways in, one source of content</h2>
        <div class="case-body">
          <p>
            The town is a Preact application inside an Astro page. The plain
            portfolio renders the same project and profile content as HTML, so
            visitors can read it without JavaScript.
          </p>
          <p>
            Quick-access buttons and project URLs bypass walking. Exploration
            adds personality, but it is optional.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="case-prose" aria-labelledby="movement-title">
        <h2 id="movement-title">Keep movement state together</h2>
        <div class="case-body">
          <p>
            Keyboard input, held touch controls and tap-to-walk all affect the
            same character. The movement hook owns position, held keys, routes
            and the animation loop. The page asks it to enter a scene or follow
            a route instead of coordinating mutable state itself.
          </p>
          <CaseFlow
            title="From a tap to a room"
            steps={[
              {
                title: "Choose a destination",
                detail: "Resolve the ground point or building",
              },
              {
                title: "Find a route",
                detail: "Check the grid and the space between steps",
              },
              {
                title: "Walk and arrive",
                detail: "Advance frames, then run the destination action",
              },
            ]}
            caption="The world defines where movement is allowed. The movement hook executes the route; the page handles the resulting interaction."
          />
          <p>
            A four-direction search on a 10-pixel grid is sufficient for this
            small, fixed world. It is straightforward to inspect and test, at
            the cost of less natural paths than free-form navigation.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="case-prose" aria-labelledby="routing-title">
        <h2 id="routing-title">Try the routing decision</h2>
        <div class="case-body town-study-demo">
          <p>
            Choose the bridge or the pond to see how the same pathfinder used by
            the town handles a reachable destination and a blocked one.
          </p>
          <RouteDemo client:only="preact" headingLevel={3}>
            <p slot="fallback">
              Enable JavaScript to try the route inspector. The{" "}
              <a href={`${source}/tests/world.unit.spec.ts`}>route tests</a>{" "}
              also show how blocked destinations and collision boundaries are
              checked.
            </p>
          </RouteDemo>
          <p>
            <a href={`${source}/src/components/town/world.ts`}>
              Pathfinding and collision code ↗
            </a>{" "}
            ·{" "}
            <a href={`${source}/src/components/town/useMovement.ts`}>
              Movement lifecycle ↗
            </a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="case-prose" aria-labelledby="navigation-title">
        <h2 id="navigation-title">Treat navigation as part of the interface</h2>
        <div class="case-body">
          <p>
            Project slugs identify content across URLs, workshop machines and
            demos. Changing the display order does not change what a link opens.
            Browser history and reloads restore the selected content.
          </p>
          <p>
            Dialogs focus their headings and return focus when closed through
            the interface. Reduced-motion preferences skip automatic travel to
            destinations. Saved discoveries are optional: malformed data is
            validated, and denied storage leaves the town usable for the current
            visit.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="case-prose" aria-labelledby="verification-title">
        <h2 id="verification-title">Test the difficult boundaries</h2>
        <div class="case-body">
          <p>
            Unit tests check collision boundaries, route segments, URL parsing
            and saved-progress validation. Browser journeys cover touch and
            keyboard input, deep links, focus, and the JavaScript-free portfolio
            across Chromium, Firefox and WebKit.
          </p>
          <p>
            Movement tests advance a controlled clock to avoid relying on a CI
            runner’s frame rate. They still exercise the real route and movement
            code.
          </p>
          <p>
            These checks do not establish that first-time visitors find
            everything easily. The next design check is to watch people find a
            project, explain what I do, and locate a contact route without
            guidance.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="case-prose" aria-labelledby="measurement-title">
        <h2 id="measurement-title">Recorded evidence</h2>
        <div class="case-body">
          <p>
            In the homepage baseline recorded on{" "}
            <time datetime={baseline.date}>{baseline.date}</time>, first
            contentful paint ranged from <strong>{range("fcpMs")}</strong> and
            the observed largest-contentful-paint candidate from{" "}
            <strong>{range("observedLcpMs")}</strong>, across three local runs.
          </p>
          <p>
            The measurement used revision{" "}
            <code>{baseline.measuredRevision}</code>, a 390 × 844 viewport, 4×
            CPU slowdown, 150 ms network latency and a 200,000-byte/s download
            limit. Each run used a fresh browser context; observation ended
            three seconds after load.
          </p>
          <p>
            This is a historical local baseline, not a current production
            measurement or evidence of improved hiring outcomes.
          </p>
          <p>
            <a href={`${source}/reports/portfolio-baseline.json`}>
              Read the recorded baseline ↗
            </a>{" "}
            ·{" "}
            <a href={`${source}/scripts/measure-pages.mjs`}>
              Measurement script ↗
            </a>
          </p>
          <p>
            <a href={`${source}/tests/navigation.spec.ts`}>
              History and focus checks ↗
            </a>{" "}
            ·{" "}
            <a href={`${source}/tests/world.unit.spec.ts`}>
              Collision and route checks ↗
            </a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <footer class="case-contact">
        <img src="/profile.jpeg" width="319" height="319" alt="" />
        <div>
          <h2>From a personal project to production work</h2>
          <p>
            For frontend architecture and performance in a commercial product,
            read the KTO case study.
          </p>
          <a href="/work/kto">Read the KTO case study ↗</a>
          <p>
            <a href={`mailto:${profile.email}`}>Email Jack ↗</a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </footer>
    </main>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .town-study-demo :global(.project-demo) {
    margin-top: var(--content-section-space);
    margin-bottom: var(--content-text-space);
  }
  .town-study-demo :global(.project-demo h3) {
    margin: 0 0 12px;
    font-size: 18px;
  }
  .town-study-preview {
    max-width: 800px;
    margin: var(--content-section-space) 0;
  }
  .town-study-preview img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border-radius: var(--radius-panel);
  }
  .town-study-preview figcaption {
    margin-top: 10px;
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }
</style>
